speechtext:
The reply to Mr. Cossitt's question No. 1,489 stated, in part, that the firm of Lang, Michener & Co. of Toronto were paid $8,124.34 for work performed as legal agents. In researching the answer to question No. 1,830, it was determined that Lang, Michener & Co. did not act as agents of the Crown but rather they acted as the solicitors for the plaintiffs (the expropriated owners). The $8,124.34 was in fact a payment by the Crown of legal costs reasonably incurred by the expropriated owner in asserting a claim for compensation under the Expropriation Act. As a result of a clerical error, the $8,124.34 was incorrectly recorded as an agent's fee.
Consequently, the answers to the above question are:
(a)	Nil;
(b)	Nil;
(c)	Nil;
(d)	Nil.

Preliminary data indicate that the numbers of public servants retiring with pensions in October, November and December of 1980 were as follows:
(a)	October-466;
(b)	November-549;
(c)	December-1,937.
All of the foregoing would be eligible for the 9.7 per cent increase commencing in January, 1981, under the terms of the Supplementary Retirement Benefits Act.
